/*!
    \page style-widget-reference.html
    \title Widget Style Reference
    \brief The style elements, state flags, and style options that each Qt
    widget uses.

    This reference describes how Qt's widgets use the style. Use it when you
    implement a style, to see which elements you need to draw and which
    information the widgets pass to you, or when you write a style-aware widget
    or a delegate, to draw the same elements that Qt's widgets draw.

    The reference is split into three pages:

    \list
    \li \l{Styling Buttons and Input Widgets}: push buttons, checkboxes and
        radio buttons, tool buttons, combo boxes, spin boxes, sliders, scroll
        bars, and progress bars.
    \li \l{Styling Containers and Windows}: tab widgets, group boxes, splitters,
        toolboxes, toolbars, dock widgets, title bars, size grips, and rubber
        bands.
    \li \l{Styling Menus and Item Views}: menus, menu bars, item view headers,
        tree branch indicators, and item view items.
    \endlist

    \section1 What the reference lists for each widget

    \list
    \li \b{The element tree.} The style elements that the widget draws, in
        drawing order. A nested element is drawn by the element above it in
        \l QCommonStyle. Next to each element, the reference names the sub
        element rectangles, subcontrols, and pixel metrics that \l QCommonStyle
        uses when it draws that element. Your own style can structure its
        drawing differently; only the elements the widget calls are fixed.
    \li \b{A screenshot.} The widget in the Fusion style, with the bounding
        rectangles of the elements outlined. The colors only tell the rectangles
        apart. Rectangles that coincide are drawn one pixel inside each other.
    \li \b{The state flags.} The \l QStyle::StateFlag values that the widget
        sets on its style option, in addition to the common flags that
        \l QStyleOption::initFrom() sets.
    \li \b{The style option members.} The members of the widget's
        \l QStyleOption subclass and what the widget stores in them.
    \endlist

    The common state flags and members are described in
    \l{How a Style Draws a Widget#Common state flags and members}{How a Style
    Draws a Widget}.

    \section1 What the reference leaves out

    The widgets also use the style for calculations that aren't part of drawing.
    They ask for their size hints with \l{QStyle::}{sizeFromContents()}, for
    their frame widths and margins with \l{QStyle::}{pixelMetric()}, and for the
    position of their subelements with \l{QStyle::}{subElementRect()}. The
    reference names the contents types and the pixel metrics that each widget
    queries but doesn't describe how the built-in styles calculate them. How a
    style performs those calculations is up to the style. Qt doesn't impose a
    structure. When in doubt, read the \l QCommonStyle implementation.

    \sa {Styles and Style Aware Widgets}, {How a Style Draws a Widget}, QStyle,
        QStyleOption
*/
